  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 156 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nTuesday through Sunday:\n\nThrough the rest of the work week high pressure over the central\nand eastern CONUS will be near stagnant. Its glacial pace\neastward will keep conditions dry and light southerly to light\nand variable through the week. With the sustained southerly\nwinds, temperatures will warm a bit with highs in the upper 60s\nto low 70s and overnight lows in the mid 40s to low 50s. A lake\nbreeze is expected to move inland most days. \n\nLooking ahead to the weekend is where there looks to be a\npotential break in the constant high pressure and dry weather. A\nlow pressure system is expected to move from the northern\nRockies into Saskatchewan and Ontario. Southerly winds and \ncloud cover should increase across the state ahead of this low \npressure Saturday. Confidence on the timing, strength and \nplacement of the low is still low. There is a lot of \ndisagreement among models and this would impact whether or not a\nfrontal boundary moves down Wisconsin as the low pressure \npasses to our north. Even if the frontal boundary moves through \nover the weekend, there is a question on if there will even be \nenough moisture to get rain. With a lot still up in the air POPs\nare very low during this time around 5-15%.\n\nPatterson",
